              "kind": "text",
              "section": "2.1",
              "subsection": "2.1.6",
              "rawText": "We say that two ordered n-tuples are equal if and only if each corresponding pair of their\nelements is equal. In other words, (a 1,a 2,… ,a n) = (b1,b 2,… ,b n) if and only if ai = bi,f o r\ni = 1, 2,… ,n . In particular, ordered 2-tuples are called ordered pairs. The ordered pairs (a, b)\nand (c, d) are equal if and only ifa = c and b = d. Note that (a, b) and (b, a) are not equal unless\na = b.",
              "text": "We say that two ordered n-tuples are equal if and only if each corresponding pair of their elements is equal. In other words, (a 1,a 2,… ,a n) = (b1,b 2,… ,b n) if and only if ai = bi,f o r i = 1, 2,… ,n . In particular, ordered 2-tuples are called ordered pairs. The ordered pairs (a, b) and (c, d) are equal if and only ifa = c and b = d. Note that (a, b) and (b, a) are not equal unless a = b.",
