              "id": "rosen-ch2-text-1716-1725",
              "kind": "text",
              "section": "2.3",
              "subsection": "2.3.2",
              "rawText": "For future reference, we summarize what needs be to shown to establish whether a function\nis one-to-one and whether it is onto. It is instructive to review Examples 8–17 in light of this\nsummary.\nSuppose that f : A → B.\nTo show that f is injective Show that if f (x) = f (y) for arbitrary x, y∈A, then x = y.\nTo show that f is not injective Find particular elements x, y∈A such that x ≠ y and f (x) =\nf (y).\nTo show that f is surjective Consider an arbitrary element y ∈B and ﬁnd an element x ∈A\nsuch that f (x) = y.\nTo show that f is not surjective Find a particular y ∈B such that f (x) ≠ y for all x ∈A.",
              "text": "For future reference, we summarize what needs be to shown to establish whether a function is one-to-one and whether it is onto. It is instructive to review Examples 8–17 in light of this summary. Suppose that f : A → B. To show that f is injective Show that if f (x) = f (y) for arbitrary x, y∈A, then x = y. To show that f is not injective Find particular elements x, y∈A such that x ≠ y and f (x) = f (y). To show that f is surjective Consider an arbitrary element y ∈B and find an element x ∈A such that f (x) = y. To show that f is not surjective Find a particular y ∈B such that f (x) ≠ y for all x ∈A.",
