              "kind": "example",
              "section": "2.3",
              "subsection": "2.3.3",
              "rawText": "EXAMPLE 19 Let f be the function from {a, b, c}to {1, 2, 3} such that f (a) = 2, f (b) = 3, and f (c) = 1. Is f\ninvertible, and if it is, what is its inverse?\nSolution: The function f is invertible because it is a one-to-one correspondence. The in-\nverse function f−1 reverses the correspondence given by f ,s of −1(1) = c, f−1(2) = a, and f−1\n(3) = b. ◂",
