              "id": "rosen-ch2-text-1909-1916",
              "kind": "text",
              "section": "2.3",
              "subsection": "2.3.5",
              "rawText": "We display the graphs of the ﬂoor and ceiling functions in Figure 10. In Figure 10(a) we display\nthe graph of the ﬂoor function ⌊x⌋. Note that this function has the same value throughout the\ninterval [n, n+ 1), namely n, and then it jumps up to n+ 1 when x = n+ 1. In Figure 10(b)\nwe display the graph of the ceiling function ⌈x⌉. Note that this function has the same valueLinks throughout the interval (n, n+ 1], namely n+ 1, and then jumps ton+ 2 when x is a little larger\nthan n+ 1.\nThe ﬂoor and ceiling functions are useful in a wide variety of applications, including those\ninvolving data storage and data transmission. Consider Examples 29 and 30, typical of basic\ncalculations done when database and data communications problems are studied.",
              "text": "We display the graphs of the floor and ceiling functions in Figure 10. In Figure 10(a) we display the graph of the floor function ⌊x⌋. Note that this function has the same value throughout the interval [n, n+ 1), namely n, and then it jumps up to n+ 1 when x = n+ 1. In Figure 10(b) we display the graph of the ceiling function ⌈x⌉. Note that this function has the same valueLinks throughout the interval (n, n+ 1], namely n+ 1, and then jumps ton+ 2 when x is a little larger than n+ 1. The floor and ceiling functions are useful in a wide variety of applications, including those involving data storage and data transmission. Consider Examples 29 and 30, typical of basic calculations done when database and data communications problems are studied.",
