              "kind": "proof",
              "section": "2.3",
              "subsection": "2.3.5",
              "rawText": "Proof: Suppose that ⌊x⌋ = m, where m is a positive integer. By property (1a), it follows that\nm ≤ x < m + 1. Adding n to all three quantities in this chain of two inequalities shows that\nm + n ≤ x + n < m + n + 1. Using property (1a) again, we see that⌊x + n⌋ = m + n = ⌊x⌋ + n.\nThis completes the proof. Proofs of the other properties are left as exercises.",
              "text": "Proof: Suppose that ⌊x⌋ = m, where m is a positive integer. By property (1a), it follows that m ≤ x < m + 1. Adding n to all three quantities in this chain of two inequalities shows that m + n ≤ x + n < m + n + 1. Using property (1a) again, we see that⌊x + n⌋ = m + n = ⌊x⌋ + n. This completes the proof. Proofs of the other properties are left as exercises.",
