              "solution": "To attack this problem, we begin by looking at the difference of consecutive terms, but we do not see a pattern. When we form the ratio of consecutive terms to see whether each term is a multiple of the previous term, we find that this ratio, although not a constant, is close to 3. So it is reasonable to suspect that the terms of this sequence are generated by a formula involving 3 n. Comparing these terms with the corresponding terms of the sequence {3n},w e notice that the nth term is 2 less than the corresponding power of 3. We see thatan = 3n −2f o r 1 ≤ n ≤ 10 and conjecture that this formula holds for all n. ◂",
