              "kind": "text",
              "section": "2.4",
              "subsection": "2.4.5",
              "rawText": "Next, we consider the addition of the terms of a sequence. For this we introduce summation\nnotation. We begin by describing the notation used to express the sum of the terms\nam,a m+1,… ,a n\nfrom the sequence{an}. We use the notation\nn∑\nj= m\naj , ∑n\nj= m aj , or ∑\nm≤j≤n aj\n(read as the sum from j = m to j = n of aj) to represent\nam + am+1 + ⋯ + an.\nHere, the variablej is called theindex of summation, and the choice of the letterj as the variable\nis arbitrary; that is, we could have used any other letter, such asi or k. Or, in notation,\nn∑\nj= m\naj =\nn∑\ni= m\nai =\nn∑\nk= m\nak.",
              "text": "Next, we consider the addition of the terms of a sequence. For this we introduce summation notation. We begin by describing the notation used to express the sum of the terms am,a m+1,… ,a n from the sequence{an}. We use the notation n∑ j= m aj , ∑n j= m aj , or ∑ m≤j≤n aj (read as the sum from j = m to j = n of aj) to represent am + am+1 + ⋯ + an. Here, the variablej is called theindex of summation, and the choice of the letterj as the variable is arbitrary; that is, we could have used any other letter, such asi or k. Or, in notation, n∑ j= m aj = n∑ i= m ai = n∑ k= m ak.",
