              "subsection": "2.4.5",
              "rawText": "Proof: Let\nSn =\nn∑\nj=0\narj.\nTo compute S, ﬁrst multiply both sides of the equality by r and then manipulate the resulting\nsum as follows:\nrSn = r\nn∑\nj=0\narj substituting summation formula for S\n=\nn∑\nj=0\narj+1 by the distributive property\n=\nn+1∑\nk=1\nark shifting the index of summation, with k = j+ 1\n=\n( n∑\nk=0\nark\n)\n+ (arn+1 −a) removing k = n+ 1 term and adding k = 0t e r m\n= Sn + (arn+1 −a) substituting S for summation formula\nFrom these equalities, we see that\nrSn = Sn + (arn+1 −a).\nSolving for Sn shows that if r ≠ 1, then\nSn = arn+1 −a\nr−1 .\nIf r = 1, then the Sn = ∑n\nj=0 arj = ∑n\nj=0\na = (n+ 1)a.",
              "text": "Proof: Let Sn = n∑ j=0 arj. To compute S, first multiply both sides of the equality by r and then manipulate the resulting sum as follows: rSn = r n∑ j=0 arj substituting summation formula for S = n∑ j=0 arj+1 by the distributive property = n+1∑ k=1 ark shifting the index of summation, with k = j+ 1 = ( n∑ k=0 ark ) + (arn+1 −a) removing k = n+ 1 term and adding k = 0t e r m = Sn + (arn+1 −a) substituting S for summation formula From these equalities, we see that rSn = Sn + (arn+1 −a). Solving for Sn shows that if r ≠ 1, then Sn = arn+1 −a r−1 . If r = 1, then the Sn = ∑n j=0 arj = ∑n j=0 a = (n+ 1)a.",
