              "kind": "text",
              "section": "2.5",
              "subsection": "2.5.3",
              "rawText": "Because Theorem 2 seems to be quite straightforward, we might expect that it has an easy\nproof. However, this is not the case because when you have an injection from a set A to a set\nB that may not be onto, and another injection from B to A that also may not be onto, there\nis no obvious way to construct a bijection from A to B. Moreover, even though it has been\nproved in a variety of ways without using advanced mathematics, all known proofs are rather\nsubtle and have twists and turns that are tricky to explain. One of these proofs is developed",
              "text": "Because Theorem 2 seems to be quite straightforward, we might expect that it has an easy proof. However, this is not the case because when you have an injection from a set A to a set B that may not be onto, and another injection from B to A that also may not be onto, there is no obvious way to construct a bijection from A to B. Moreover, even though it has been proved in a variety of ways without using advanced mathematics, all known proofs are rather subtle and have twists and turns that are tricky to explain. One of these proofs is developed",
