        "id": "rosen-ch2-definition-2-3-13",
        "kind": "definition",
        "section": "2.3",
        "subsection": "2.3.6",
        "rawText": "Deﬁnition 13 A partial function f from a set A to a set B is an assignment to each element a in a subset\nof A, called the domain of deﬁnition of f , of a unique element b in B. The sets A and B are\ncalled the domain and codomain of f , respectively. We say that f is undeﬁned for elements\nin A that are not in the domain of deﬁnition of f . When the domain of deﬁnition of f equals\nA, we say that f is a total function.",
        "text": "Definition 13 A partial function f from a set A to a set B is an assignment to each element a in a subset of A, called the domain of definition of f , of a unique element b in B. The sets A and B are called the domain and codomain of f , respectively. We say that f is undefined for elements in A that are not in the domain of definition of f . When the domain of definition of f equals A, we say that f is a total function.",
