          "kind": "exercise-context",
          "section": "2.3",
          "subsection": "2.3.6",
          "rawText": "Let f be a function from the set A to the set B.L e tS be a\nsubset of B. We deﬁne the inverse image of S to be the sub-\nset of A whose elements are precisely all preimages of all\nelements of S.W ed e n o t et h ei n v e r s ei m a g eo fSby f−1(S),\nso f−1(S) ={ a ∈A ∣f (a) ∈S}.[ Beware: The notation f−1is\nused in two diﬀerent ways. Do not confuse the notation intro-\nduced here with the notation f−1(y) for the value at y of the\ninverse of the invertible function f . Notice also that f−1(S),\nthe inverse image of the setS, makes sense for all functionsf ,\nnot just invertible functions.]",
          "text": "Let f be a function from the set A to the set B.L e tS be a subset of B. We define the inverse image of S to be the subset of A whose elements are precisely all preimages of all elements of S.W ed e n o t et h ei n v e r s ei m a g eo fSby f−1(S), so f−1(S) ={ a ∈A ∣f (a) ∈S}.[ Beware: The notation f−1is used in two different ways. Do not confuse the notation introduced here with the notation f−1(y) for the value at y of the inverse of the invertible function f . Notice also that f−1(S), the inverse image of the setS, makes sense for all functionsf , not just invertible functions.]",
